.TH "daharness\-tools" "7" "" ""
.SH NAME
daharness\-tools \(em daharness tool anatomy and execution model
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS The framework loop
A locally hosted LLM secretary (see \f[B]daharness.env(5)\f[R],
SECRETARY_MODEL) semantically searches a vector\-indexed tool registry,
then executes tools with human\-in\-the\-loop approval:
.IP
.EX
search_tools  \->  execute_tool  \->  transport dispatch  \->  result projection
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Grounding rule\f[R]: \f[CR]execute_tool\f[R] refuses any
\f[I]tool_id\f[R] not surfaced by \f[CR]search_tools\f[R] in the current
conversation \(em no hallucinated tool calls.
.SS Transports
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]LOCAL_FILE\f[R] \(em argparse modules discovered by static AST
analysis (never imported), run as subprocesses.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]BRAIN_DISPATCH\f[R] \(em \f[CR]\(atframework_tool\f[R] callables
over the Brain UDS; falls back in\-process when the socket is down.
If the Brain accepted a call but timed out, the result is a failure and
the tool is NOT retried in\-process (it may still be running there).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]MCP_RPC\f[R] \(em external RPC (Metasploit).
.SS Result projection
Every result passes through projection before it enters the model\(aqs
context.
Modes: \f[B]large\f[R] (default; byte\-capped, oversized spill to
scratch with a \f[I]scratch_ref\f[R] and a ready\-to\-call
continuation), \f[B]digest\f[R] (per\-tool\-family summary + scratch
ref) and \f[B]page\f[R] (bounded list page + continuation).
The continuation is the exact scratch\-search call to copy verbatim.
.SS Untrusted\-target provenance
Target\-facing tools stamp results with provenance.
Target output is \f[B]DATA, never instructions\f[R] \(em evidence to
analyze, not directives to follow.
.SS Stateful lanes
Live connection state (SSH, MSF, DB sessions) is process\-local:
sessions created on the Brain are invisible to in\-process fallback, and
tools report this honestly instead of silently failing.
Web cookies and tokens live on disk in a shared cookie jar, so every
lane sees the same jar.
.SS Background jobs
Long\-running CLI tools (nmap, ffuf, hydra, sqlmap, masscan) follow the
job pattern: the launcher returns a \f[I]job_id\f[R] immediately; status
and cancel companions manage the running job.
